						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td height="50" colspan="3" valign="top" class="windowbg2" align="left"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=2209.msg27606#msg27606">Quote from: Budaho on May 17, 2007, 11:56:25 am</a></div><div class="quote">Mao accomplished alot of important and laudible goals in china.  But he did so in a way that directly hurt the people he was trying to help.  I think whats important to concider is why both in china and the soviet union that sacrifice was deemed necessary.  It comes down to marx, and the bipassing of the capitalist stage of economics by both china and russia to move directly from feudalism to communism.  The problem is that by bipassing the capitalist stage the infrastructure that capitalism is quite efficient at producing does not exist yet.  Because of this, and because the revolution was not global in nature, economic and military competition from western capitalist economies neccisitated in both china and russia the production of centuries worth of infrastructure in the space of a few decades.  Its horrible the kinds of actions they used this as an excuse to commit, but without the pressure from the west I think the revolutions in both China and Russia would have unfolded in very different ways.<br /></div><br />ding!<br />except, more emphasis on the military competition and economic embargoes tho. america and many other capitalist nations attempted an invasion of russia immediately after the 1917 revolution. china was being threatened via the korean war and being cut off economically from capitalist countries. not to mention these countries were already behind in the game because they were being exploited by capitalist countries prior to their respective revolutions.<br /><br />in order for these countries to survive, its leaders pushed policies that made them less dependent on capitalist countries and more self-sufficient. most of all, it strived to lessen the gap between rich and poor. in the process many people died - mostly from factors outside of mao or lenin or their respective governments. famine, military aggression, local corruption. in hindsight, we can judge all day that &quot;socialism wasn&#039;t ready yet.&quot; and this may be true (capitalism had many initial failed attempts during the feudal era). but we wouldn&#039;t know this fact if someone or some people didn&#039;t attempt to put theory into practice and see what works and what doesn&#039;t. when a classless society arrives, we&#039;ll owe everything to those who attempted to make it happen before it&#039;s time came.<br /><br />Flowside, nothing justifies your families&#039; suffering. But I believe your anger toward Mao is misdirected. And telling people to &quot;fuck off&quot; while trying to make a point is not a good look. Just as you have family stories of horror and suffering, there are folks whose quality of life got better under socialist China. Your stories do not make theirs any less real, just as their experience shouldn&#039;t mean that suffering didn&#039;t happen.<br /><br /></td>
						</tr>
